WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.442 --> 00:00:12.565
de Cervantes Saavedra is the author of one of the world's most famous novels, Don Quixote de la

2
00:00:12.605 --> 00:00:18.867
Mancha. This is the story of a country gentleman who decides to become a knight after reading many

3
00:00:18.947 --> 00:00:22.709
romantic tales about knights in armor and highborn ladies.

4
00:00:23.549 --> 00:00:31.164
This gentleman takes the name Don Quixote, and he and his down-to-earth squire, Sancho Panza, go on quests

5
00:00:31.244 --> 00:00:33.545
to find adventure and fight injustice.

6
00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:37.006
This simple story changed literature forever.

7
00:00:37.946 --> 00:00:44.968
Cervantes was born in 1547 outside of Madrid, Spain, in the city of Alcalá de Henares.

8
00:00:45.628 --> 00:00:47.069
He was one of seven children.

9
00:00:47.689 --> 00:00:53.170
Not much is known of his early childhood, but one thing is certain—he was an eager reader of

10
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:59.532
books. In the fall of 1569, young Cervantes went to Rome to work for the Catholic Church.

11
00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:05.669
Some claim he really went to Rome to escape arrest for having wounded another man in a duel.

12
00:01:06.449 --> 00:01:13.035
Whatever the reason, being in Rome allowed him to study art, philosophy, and literature and to experience the

13
00:01:13.095 --> 00:01:19.140
many changes happening in society during the transition from medieval times to the early Renaissance.

14
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.404
Scientific thinking was on the rise and traditional knowledge was being challenged.

15
00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:31.246
Cosmopolitan areas, where people with different beliefs and experiences tended to come together, were expanding.

16
00:01:32.427 --> 00:01:37.051
In 1570, when he was 24, Cervantes joined the Spanish army.

17
00:01:37.711 --> 00:01:43.877
At that time, the Muslim Turks of the Ottoman Empire were fighting European Christians for control of the

18
00:01:43.917 --> 00:01:47.500
Mediterranean, and Cervantes wanted to be a part of the fight.

19
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:54.183
He joined the Holy League, a mighty armada of three hundred ships and the Catholic armies of Pope

20
00:01:54.223 --> 00:01:59.285
Pius V, Venice, and Spain, which totaled about eighty thousand men.

21
00:02:00.006 --> 00:02:06.268
In 1571, Cervantes was on a ship near Greece when he was shot three times in the Battle

22
00:02:06.328 --> 00:02:07.009
of Lepanto.

23
00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:10.590
One bullet hit him in the left hand, crippling it.

24
00:02:11.271 --> 00:02:13.632
He was never able to use that hand again.

25
00:02:14.356 --> 00:02:20.702
The battle was a huge victory for the Holy League, and Cervantes later wrote, "On that day, Ottoman

26
00:02:20.742 --> 00:02:22.404
pride and arrogance were broken."

27
00:02:23.425 --> 00:02:29.350
Cervantes served for four more years, fighting in several more military campaigns against the Turks.

28
00:02:29.951 --> 00:02:37.298
He was promoted and was now receiving a high salary; nevertheless, tired of war, Cervantes decided to return

29
00:02:37.358 --> 00:02:38.379
to Spain in 1575.

30
00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.545
That September, he boarded a galley named El Sol in Naples, Italy.

31
00:02:45.145 --> 00:02:51.332
The ship was attacked by Turkish pirates, and Cervantes and the other survivors of the attack were taken

32
00:02:51.392 --> 00:02:52.893
to Algiers as captives.

33
00:02:53.554 --> 00:03:00.100
A city on the coast of North Africa, Algiers was the center of Turkish piracy, where supplies, stolen

34
00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.344
merchandise, and slaves were freely bought and sold.

35
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.263
Prisoners were treated like slaves, forced to work all day and night with little food, water, or rest.

36
00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:12.144
Many died in captivity.

37
00:03:12.664 --> 00:03:17.306
If a slave was wealthy, pirates would attempt to get a ransom from the person's family.

38
00:03:17.986 --> 00:03:24.329
Because Cervantes had letters of recommendation from important people in the Spanish army, the Turks thought he must

39
00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:25.730
be a valuable captive.

40
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:31.092
Even though Cervantes told them he had no money, the Turks did not believe him.

41
00:03:31.932 --> 00:03:37.757
They decided to ransom him for five hundred gold coins, a sum his family could hardly hope to

42
00:03:37.797 --> 00:03:43.060
raise. Cervantes made four escape attempts but was captured every time.

43
00:03:43.881 --> 00:03:49.285
After five years in captivity, Cervantes' ransom was finally paid by a group of friars.

44
00:03:50.046 --> 00:03:56.570
Cervantes returned home to Spain in 1580, but his long imprisonment had left an enduring mark on him

45
00:03:56.731 --> 00:03:58.312
as a man and as a writer.

46
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.923
As soon as he reached home, Cervantes started his writing career in earnest.

47
00:04:04.064 --> 00:04:10.448
In 1585, a few months after getting married, Cervantes published the romantic novel La Galatea.

48
00:04:11.349 --> 00:04:15.992
Some of his plays were being performed in theaters in Madrid as well, but they did not provide

49
00:04:16.052 --> 00:04:18.914
Cervantes with much money to take care of his household.

50
00:04:20.015 --> 00:04:28.356
So, in 1587, Cervantes accepted a government position requisitioning grain and other supplies for the Spanish Armada, a

51
00:04:28.416 --> 00:04:32.218
fleet of over one hundred ships being prepared to battle England.

52
00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:41.243
After the armada was destroyed in 1588 by Elizabeth I and her navy—a horrible embarrassment for Spain—Cervantes became

53
00:04:41.283 --> 00:04:46.626
a tax collector, going from town to town and collecting rents and taxes for the government.

54
00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:52.809
Suspicion that he was falsifying his accounts got him in trouble with the government, and he wound up

55
00:04:52.869 --> 00:04:57.692
in jail twice over the course of six years for financial impropriety.

56
00:04:58.428 --> 00:05:03.431
Many scholars, however, believe that he was honest but simply a bad businessman.

57
00:05:04.031 --> 00:05:08.954
Cervantes later said that part of his masterpiece Don Quixote was written in prison.

58
00:05:10.235 --> 00:05:15.658
In 1605, at the age of 58, Cervantes published the first part of Don Quixote.

59
00:05:16.298 --> 00:05:23.502
The novel was a huge success, one he badly needed after years of intense hardship and financial insecurity.

60
00:05:24.182 --> 00:05:29.590
Ten years later, he published the second part, which some critics think surpasses the first.

61
00:05:30.271 --> 00:05:36.356
Cervantes had lost much of his idealism about the Spanish Empire and the romance of battle, and in

62
00:05:36.396 --> 00:05:40.740
Don Quixote, he makes fun of everyone—nobility and commoners.

63
00:05:41.361 --> 00:05:47.947
His main character, Don Quixote, bases his life's purpose on upholding the "truth" that he has read about

64
00:05:48.007 --> 00:05:49.228
in romantic stories.

65
00:05:50.428 --> 00:05:56.993
Don Quixote does battle with windmills, thinking they are giants, and mistakes a farm girl for a wellborn

66
00:05:57.073 --> 00:05:58.654
lady needing his protection.

67
00:05:59.434 --> 00:06:04.057
When he comes to a small inn, the knight thinks it is the castle of a great lord.

68
00:06:04.698 --> 00:06:11.102
Sancho Panza constantly tries to get his master to see things as they really are, but Don Quixote

69
00:06:11.222 --> 00:06:13.924
insists on seeing the world in magical ways.

70
00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:21.163
Readers everywhere were touched by both the humor and the sadness of Cervantes' depiction of the human experience.

71
00:06:21.843 --> 00:06:26.385
The story is a comedy that makes fun of idealistic and impractical people.

72
00:06:27.225 --> 00:06:32.907
But at the same time, it is a tragedy about a troubled man struggling with self-delusion and social

73
00:06:32.947 --> 00:06:38.790
change. It remains up to the reader to decide whether Don Quixote is a hero or a fool.

74
00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:46.706
Cervantes wrote many poems, novels, short stories, and plays, but none became as famous as Don Quixote.

75
00:06:47.487 --> 00:06:52.692
The book about the wayward knight is thought by many scholars to be the basis of the modern

76
00:06:52.732 --> 00:06:58.537
novel. Before Cervantes, most stories were written to convey unchanging moral "truths."

77
00:06:59.197 --> 00:07:06.264
Cervantes broke that tradition and created a completely new type of literature, which questions what is real and

78
00:07:06.324 --> 00:07:10.768
what is not through characters who are themselves undecided about reality.

79
00:07:11.368 --> 00:07:17.234
Not only did Cervantes help invent a new kind of story, but he also inspired a new word.

80
00:07:17.755 --> 00:07:24.041
We use this word today to sum up a person or plan that is idealistic, impulsive, and romantic:

81
00:07:24.562 --> 00:07:25.162
quixotic.
